#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166629 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166791 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f04980f8-35db-5cf4-8a4d-8e9f0f23fb1e" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Hyperbolic Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "b6135ab8-d329-5646-aeb7-dea8b38e7606" :question/math-academy-id "e-8420" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}$\nEvaluate $\\displaystyle \\int _{-1}^{1} \\coth{x}\\sinh{x}\\,\\textrm d x$." :question/worked-solution "$$\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity\n\n$$\n\\cot{x}=\\dfrac{\\cos{x}}{\\sin{x}}\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\coth{x}=\\dfrac{\\cosh{x}}{\\sinh{x}}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral using the above identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{-1}^{1}\\coth x\\sinh xdx &= \\int_{-1}^{1}\\frac{\\cosh x}{\\sinh x} \\cdot \\sinh xdx \\\\\n&= \\int_{-1}^{1}\\cosh xdx \\\\\n&= \\left.\\sinh x\\right|_{-1}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\sinh (1) - \\sinh (-1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{e^{1} - e^{-1}}{2} - (e^{-1} - e^{- (-1)})/(2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{e^{1} - e^{-1} - e^{-1} + e^{1}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2e - 2e^{-1}}{2} \\\\\n&= e - e^{-1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166630 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e14a3673-7b90-5f81-b73a-c2894f07c561" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Hyperbolic Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "555cf939-5320-5230-bcef-4828210fbc34" :question/math-academy-id "e-8421" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\tanh}{\\mathop{\\rm tanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int _{-1}^{1} \\dfrac{\\tanh^2{x}}{2\\sinh^2 x} \\,\\textrm d x$." :question/worked-solution "$$\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\tanh}{\\mathop{\\rm tanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int _{-1}^{1} \\dfrac{\\tanh^2{x}}{2\\sinh^2 x}\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\int _{-1}^{1} \\dfrac{1}{\\sinh^2{x}}\\cdot \\tanh^2{x} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nNow we need to find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity\n\n$$\n\\tan{x}=\\dfrac{\\sin{x}}{\\cos{x}}\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\tanh{x} = \\dfrac{\\sinh{x}}{\\cosh{x}}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identity to write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}\\int_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sinh^{2} x} \\cdot \\tanh^{2} xdx &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sinh^{2} x} \\cdot \\frac{\\sinh^{2} x}{\\cosh^{2} x}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{-1}^{1}\\frac{\\sinh^{2} x}{\\sinh^{2} x} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cosh^{2} x}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\cosh^{2} x}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{-1}^{1}sech^{2} x \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{1}{2}\\tanh x\\right|_{-1}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\tanh (1) - \\tanh (-1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{e - e^{-1}}{e + e^{-1}} - \\frac{e^{-1} - e}{e^{-1} + e}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{e^{2} - 1}{e^{2} + 1} - \\frac{1 - e^{2}}{1 + e^{2}}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(2 \\cdot \\frac{e^{2} - 1}{e^{2} + 1}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{e^{2} - 1}{e^{2} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166631 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7fafc0a-8fff-5c9d-a55a-f25ce3c1d729" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Hyperbolic Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "27ef207b-1337-5a3b-92cc-0d7a8b6bb13e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8422" :question/problem "Evaluate\n$\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\displaystyle{\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\ln 3} \\tanh x \\, \\textrm{d}x}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\tan{x} = \\dfrac{\\sin{x}}{\\cos{x}}\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\tanh{x} = \\dfrac{\\sinh{x}}{\\cosh{x}}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{\\ln 3}\\tanh xdx &= \\int_{0}^{\\ln 3}\\frac{\\sinh x}{\\cosh x}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let\n\n$$\nu = \\cosh{x}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\sinh{x} \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad \\textrm{d}u = \\sinh{x} \\, \\textrm d x\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration according to the rule\n\n$$\nu = \\cosh{x}\n$$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\ln 3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac{5}{3}$ |\n\nNow, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{\\ln 3}\\frac{\\sinh x}{\\cosh x}dx &= \\int_{1}^{5/3}\\frac{1}{u}du \\\\\n&= \\left.\\ln \\mid u \\mid \\right|_{1}^{5/3} \\\\\n&= \\ln (\\frac{5}{3}) - \\ln 1 \\\\\n&= \\ln (\\frac{5}{3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166725 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c7ddaa7-f225-56f3-966a-f214152b1ad0" :knowledge-point/title "Deriving a Hyperbolic Identity Using Osborn's Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "e05932ad-3ee7-5b8a-a0c9-8e8ac50990d0" :question/math-academy-id "e-9881" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}$Find a hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity $1 + \\cot^2{x} = \\csc^2{x}$." :question/worked-solution "$$\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nOsborn's rule allows us to construct a hyperbolic identity from a corresponding trigonometric identity.\n\nTo apply Osborn's rule, we follow two steps:\n\n1. Replace every trigonometric function with its corresponding hyperbolic function.\n2. For any product of two trigonometric sines, flip the sign of the corresponding product of hyperbolic sines.\n\nWe're given the following trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n1 + \\cot^2{x} = \\csc^2{x}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\cot^2{x} = \\dfrac{\\cos^2{x}}{\\sin^2{x}} \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\csc^2{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin^2{x}}\n$$\n\nand thus, we have two products of sines. Therefore, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n1 \\,{\\mathbf{\\color{red}{-}}}\\, \\coth^2{x} = {\\mathbf{\\color{red}{-}}}\\,\\csch^2{x}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167400 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166791 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f04980f8-35db-5cf4-8a4d-8e9f0f23fb1e" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Hyperbolic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8abbc701-fd57-5e8f-a04a-2e363418c822" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165609} {:answer/id #uuid "a3027c11-4f32-54eb-af22-c8c6f931ed2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165610} {:answer/id #uuid "e6e7dc8a-e303-5ede-9956-feb0245b7a05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165611} {:answer/id #uuid "f650e3cb-270a-5d6c-b194-a6df6b50eaf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165612} {:answer/id #uuid "4c67467a-de32-5061-b17a-5a68199c51b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165613}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b66d5234-ac6b-50a2-bf9b-30debd44462c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165608}] :question/id #uuid "37030af8-6f60-5f87-96ef-c720c85ba743" :question/math-academy-id "q-95742" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\ln 2}\\tanh x\\cosh xdx$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167464 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c7ddaa7-f225-56f3-966a-f214152b1ad0" :knowledge-point/title "Deriving a Hyperbolic Identity Using Osborn's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a3b3d29-a72d-5118-9176-37fe45ac0ff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cosh^{2} x + \\sinh^{2} x = e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165993} {:answer/id #uuid "a2934816-98e9-59a6-8734-e614f52e3882" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cosh^{2} x + \\sinh^{2} x =-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165994} {:answer/id #uuid "26dd9570-dd08-50c9-8822-ffc21562e879" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cosh^{2} x - \\sinh^{2} x = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165995} {:answer/id #uuid "5950302b-7364-5c7d-848e-b73e39501d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cosh^{2} x - \\sinh^{2} x =-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165996} {:answer/id #uuid "6832a75b-5b82-5c10-be22-1f3d2289726b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cosh^{2} x + \\sinh^{2} x = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165997}] :answer-field/id #uuid "cbdfdfa3-9b3a-546a-977e-1d072d1016bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165992}] :question/id #uuid "09b00f02-3f58-5541-80d9-f11db2b14004" :question/math-academy-id "q-9628" :question/problem "Find a hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity $\\cos^{2} x + \\sin^{2} x = 1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167465 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c7ddaa7-f225-56f3-966a-f214152b1ad0" :knowledge-point/title "Deriving a Hyperbolic Identity Using Osborn's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bbc93c1-ea01-552d-b698-8215e1d64e8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sinh 2x =-2\\sinh x\\cosh x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165999} {:answer/id #uuid "a1298b58-8f94-5dda-b090-b7a71b3433c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sinh 2x = 2\\sinh x\\cosh x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166000} {:answer/id #uuid "c4038191-451e-5c4b-91bd-1275067b7351" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sinh 2x = \\sinh^{2} x - \\cosh^{2} x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166001} {:answer/id #uuid "05b6f768-cd6b-53c1-86da-f978a40e4a5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sinh 2x = \\frac{1}{2}\\sinh x\\cosh x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166002} {:answer/id #uuid "1fb86c41-3959-519f-a3d5-7d888a803337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sinh 2x = \\sinh^{2} x + \\cosh^{2} x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166003}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5299e7d7-dd38-52d1-9c80-440f5a957600" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165998}] :question/id #uuid "3747ab66-800e-539f-851f-023604b67b37" :question/math-academy-id "q-9631" :question/problem "Find a hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity $\\sin 2x = 2\\sin x\\cos x$."}]}
